What new memories came home

1. Pollination is distinct from digestion. Freebees can create value merely by transporting relational structures between bases. This gives the Metamap a new object to learn: fertile cross-domain routes.
2. Interpretation produces a self-supervising signal. Every useful transformation can output both its answer and information about how the answer was produced. Residue can therefore become a semantic error/learning signal without requiring an external label for every step.
3. Consensus needs ancestry correction. Repeated residue from highly correlated readers is weak evidence. Cross-basis recurrence becomes much more valuable when routes are genuinely independent. The Honeycomb therefore needs lineage tracking.
4. Mead has chemistry. A\rightarrow B may create something different from B\rightarrow A. Store recipes, not merely ingredients. This turns order-effects into a first-class search dimension.
5. The party itself is an operator. Participants + relations + sequence + shared context create an emergent state that none possesses individually:

\{D_i\}+\mathcal R
\rightarrow
\boxed{\Pi_{\text{party}}}.

That is the same relational-emergence primitive we just found with volume.

6. Memory can be tested as changed future behavior. If the same probe after fermentation reliably routes differently—and the change survives restoration—we have stronger evidence of an actual learned capability rather than temporary novelty.
7. Honey needs provenance or it becomes syrup. Every reusable residue should retain source, reader, basis, ruler, transformation, order, independence, Reality status, and descendants. Otherwise recombination eventually destroys our ability to tell discovery from inherited assumption.

